<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Notitia Dignitatum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Notitia Dignitatum is a late Roman document listing the civil and military offices of the eastern and western empires, compiled around 400 and updated in the west into the 420s. It records the units and commanders of each region, including the Count of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Shore and his forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and survives in illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> copies. It is a major source for the late Roman army and administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum.jpg" alt="Notitia Dignitatum" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodl_Canon.Misc.378_roll159B_frame28.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Notitia dignitatum et administrationum omnium tam civilium quam militarium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for 'List of all dignities and administrations both civil and military', details the administrative organisation of the Western and Eastern Roman empires. It is one of very few surviving documents of Roman government, and it describes several thousand offices, from the imperial court to provincial governments, diplomatic missions and army units. The text is not dated and names no author. It is usually considered accurate for the Western Empire in the 420s and for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Eastern Empire</a> in the 390s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>For each half of the empire the Notitia lists the major dignities it could bestow, often with the location and the staff, or officium, of each, except for the most junior members. The offices are arranged in three groups: court officials, including the most senior dignitaries such as the praetorian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a>; vicars and provincial governors, arranged by praetorian prefecture and diocese; and military commanders, the Comites Rei Militaris and Duces, with the full titles and stations of their regiments.</p><p>It does not record the numbers of personnel, so the size of units and of the army cannot be worked out. The late 4th-century army may have equalled that of the 2nd century, over 400,000 men, or been far smaller.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Several 15th- and 16th-century copies survive, and an illuminated one made in 1542 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-henry-elector-palatine">Otto Henry, Elector Palatine</a>, is kept in the Bavarian State Library. All derive from the Codex Spirensis, which was in the library of the chapter of Speyer Cathedral in 1542 but was lost before 1672. The most important copy was made for Pietro Donato in 1436 and illuminated by Peronet Lamy, and is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>.</p><p>As a source it has limits. The two sections were compiled at different times and may rest on data up to twenty years older, so units are often listed twice. Parts are missing through copying losses. Roger Collins calls it an archaising text written about 425 and unreliable on the presence of units in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Notitia Dignitatum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marcomanni were settled inside the empire and served throughout it: the <b>Notitia Dignitatum</b> of about 420 lists a tribune of the Marcomanni under the dux of northern Noricum and Pannonia I, palatine units of Marcomanni in Italy and Equites Marcomanni in North Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman defences against them included the forts of the Saxon Shore in Britain and on the Gallic coast, named in the <b>Notitia Dignitatum</b>, and in 367 Saxons joined Picts, Scotti and Attacotti in the attack on Britain that Ammianus Marcellinus called a barbarian conspiracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Military</a></li></ul></section>
